From: rhodep port <none@example.com>
Date: Sun, 23 Aug 2026 16:40:00 -0300
Subject: [PATCH] arm64: dts: qcom: rhodep: make the crash log survive, and
 reserve what the firmware writes

Two problems with persistent storage on this board, found while looking
for a way to diagnose the silent watchdog resets that keep mobile data
switched off.

First, the ramoops region does not come back intact. Writing a known
pattern and rebooting, 1.3% of the bytes returned altered on one boot and
3.7% on another, spread evenly through the buffer rather than gathered in
a block. Data written seconds before the reset came back as damaged as
data written three minutes earlier, so this is the reset doing it and not
retention decay. The address is not the problem: 0xaf000000 is
Motorola's own RAMOOPS_BASE_ADDR.

A console log tolerates that -- it reads fine with occasional wrong
characters. A compressed crash dump does not, and a compressed dump is
what pstore keeps by default, so every dmesg record recovered so far has
died the same way:

  pstore: zlib_inflate() failed, ret = -3!

which leaves nothing at all to read after a crash. Turn on the
Reed-Solomon ECC ramoops already implements. At ecc-size = 32 it corrects
up to sixteen bad bytes in each 255 byte block, against the three to nine
the measured rates imply.

Second, three regions listed in Motorola's memory map are written by
firmware and were never reserved here, so Linux has been handing them out
as ordinary memory: the watchdog CPU context dump, the TrustZone log
backup and the annotation buffer. The first is written at reset, when it
no longer matters. The TrustZone log backup does not have that excuse --
TZ can write it while Linux is running, over whatever the allocator gave
away.

Addresses and sizes are from include/dt-bindings/moto/moto-mem-reserve.h,
resolved: WDOG_CPUCTX_BASE is RAMOOPS_BASE_ADDR - 0x5c00 * 8, TZLOG_BCK
sits 0x30000 below that, and MMI_ANNOTATE 0x800 below again.

Signed-off-by: rhodep port <none@example.com>
---
--- a/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375-motorola-rhodep.dts
+++ b/arch/arm64/boot/dts/qcom/sm6375-motorola-rhodep.dts
@@ -126,6 +126,49 @@
 			ftrace-size = <0x0>;
 			mem-type = <1>;
 			max-reason = <5>;
+			/*
+			 * This region does not survive a reset intact. Measured
+			 * over two reboots, 1.3% and 3.7% of the bytes came back
+			 * altered, spread evenly rather than in a block, and
+			 * data written seconds before the reset was as damaged
+			 * as data written minutes earlier -- so it is the reset
+			 * that does it, not decay.
+			 *
+			 * A console log survives that: it is readable with some
+			 * characters wrong. A compressed crash dump does not,
+			 * and that is what pstore stores by default, which is
+			 * why every recovered dmesg record so far has died with
+			 * "zlib_inflate() failed, ret = -3".
+			 *
+			 * Reed-Solomon over each 255 byte block, correcting up
+			 * to ecc-size/2 bad bytes, covers both rates with room
+			 * to spare.
+			 */
+			ecc-size = <32>;
+		};
+
+		/*
+		 * Three regions the firmware writes to on its own. Motorola
+		 * reserves all of them (include/dt-bindings/moto/
+		 * moto-mem-reserve.h, sizes and addresses from there); this
+		 * port did not, so Linux has been handing them out as ordinary
+		 * memory. The TrustZone log backup is the one that matters:
+		 * TZ can write it while Linux is running, on top of whatever
+		 * happens to be there.
+		 */
+		mmi_annotate@aefa1800 {
+			reg = <0x0 0xaefa1800 0x0 0x800>;
+			no-map;
+		};
+
+		tzlog_dump@aefa2000 {
+			reg = <0x0 0xaefa2000 0x0 0x30000>;
+			no-map;
+		};
+
+		wdog_cpuctx@aefd2000 {
+			reg = <0x0 0xaefd2000 0x0 0x2e000>;
+			no-map;
 		};
 	};
 
